-- title:
--   Potentials of a Gaussian kernel: differentiation, bounds and Hölder estimates
-- statement:
--   Let $S\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ and let $K$ be a kernel on $S$ with $x$-derivatives $K_1,K_2,K_3$ satisfying the Gaussian kernel hypotheses `KHyp` of the statement (Gaussian bounds $|\partial_x^jK(t,x,y)|\le A t^{-j/2}G_{ct}(x-y)$, measurability, the heat equation $\partial_tK=\frac12\Delta_xK$ and unit mass $\int_SK(t,x,y)dy=1$). For bounded measurable $h$ put $$k_j(t,x)=\int_S h(y)\,\partial_x^jK(t,x,y)\,dy .$$ Then $K(t,x,\cdot)$ and $K_2(t,x,\cdot)$ are strongly measurable, $k_0,k_1,k_2$ are differentiable in $x$ with derivatives $k_1,k_2,k_3$, and if $|h|\le M_0$ and $|h(y)-h(z)|\le H|y-z|^\mu$ on $S$ ($0\le\mu\le2$) then, for $x\in S$, $$|k_0|\le AM_0,\quad\|k_1\|\le At^{-1/2}M_0,\quad\|k_2\|\le At^{-1}M_0,\quad\|k_j\|\le A\,t^{(\mu-j)/2}H\ (j=1,2,3),$$ together with a Lipschitz bound for $k_1$ and measurability of $t\mapsto k_2(t,x)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note**: all objects are introduced by `let` in the statement; the statement is the conjunction of the listed facts, each in the exact form used later in the proof.
-- source:
--   A. Friedman, Partial Differential Equations of Parabolic Type, Prentice-Hall, 1964, Chapter 1 (volume potentials: differentiation and Hölder estimates); D. Gilbarg and N. S. Trudinger, Elliptic Partial Differential Equations of Second Order, Springer, 2001, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61798-0, Section 4.2 (Hölder estimates for potentials); used in Stewart N. Ethier and Thomas G. Kurtz, Markov Processes: Characterization and Convergence, Wiley, 1986, https://doi.org/10.1002/9780470316658, Chapter 8, Section 1

